# ~/.shell_common.vault -- Vault helpers. TRACKED in the dotfiles repo, unlike
# ~/.shell_common.local which is deliberately machine-local. These belong here
# because nothing in them is machine-specific: the two Vault addresses are the
# same for everyone at Super.
#
# Sourced from ~/.shell_common.local, which .bashrc reads at line 16 -- ABOVE
# the interactive guard. That placement is load-bearing: ~/.shell_common is
# sourced at line 138, below the guard, so anything defined there is missing
# from `bash -c`, hooks and tool calls, and it prints a login banner. Do not
# move these into .shell_common for tidiness.

# --- drop the Vault address injection --------------------------------------
# Coder injects VAULT_ADDR=<production> at pid 1, so it is present in every
# process on this box. super_cli.util.vault.set_vault_addr_for_env() returns
# early when VAULT_ADDR is already set ("honors any user-provided override"),
# so `super vault <cmd> <service> staging` silently targets the PRODUCTION
# Vault -- whose staging/ tree is a stale copy (newest write 2026-03; the live
# staging tree is on vault-staging and was written 2026-08). The prod
# confirmation gate keys off the env argument, not the Vault reached, so it
# does not fire either.
#
# Deliberately NOT fixed by `unset VAULT_ADDR` here. Two reasons, both measured:
#   1. This file is loaded through BASH_ENV, so the unset runs on EVERY
#      non-interactive bash startup and strips VAULT_ADDR back out of any
#      child shell -- including one a helper below just set it for (observed:
#      curl exit code 000, empty URL).
#   2. Routing alone would not help. Tokens are not env-aware, so a bare
#      `super vault ... staging` would route to the staging Vault and then
#      present ~/.vault-token's PRODUCTION token, which 403s there.
# The address and the token have to move together, so pair them explicitly per
# command with the helpers below. The injected value stays as the default,
# where it is correctly paired with ~/.vault-token.

# Tokens are NOT env-aware: authenticate() takes one token from $VAULT_TOKEN
# then ~/.vault-token, and pairs it with whatever VAULT_ADDR says. Each token
# 403s against the other Vault, so addr and token must move together. Use these
# as a prefix for one command; do not export VAULT_TOKEN.
#   vault-prod    super vault get midas production
#   vault-staging super vault get midas staging
# Each reads .env.secrets fresh in a subshell rather than trusting the ambient
# environment. The _WORKSPACE_AUTH_LOADED guard above loads .env.rc once per
# process tree, so a secret ADDED or ROTATED after a long-running process
# started never reaches its children -- measured: a shell inheriting the flag
# had no VAULT_TOKEN_PROD minutes after it was added to the file.
_vault_with() {  # _vault_with <addr-var-value> <token-var-name> <cmd...>
  local addr="$1" tokvar="$2"; shift 2
  ( set -a; . /workspace/.env.secrets 2>/dev/null; set +a
    local tok="${!tokvar:-}"
    [ -n "$tok" ] || { echo "$tokvar not in /workspace/.env.secrets" >&2; return 1; }
    # VAULT_STRICT_HTTP makes hvac use GET ...?list=true instead of the
    # non-standard LIST HTTP verb. The staging Vault (or its proxy) rejects
    # LIST with 400 Bad Request while production accepts it, so without this
    # every `super vault get ... staging` dies as
    # "Vault exception InvalidRequest with args ('Bad Request',)" -- which
    # names neither the verb nor the host and reads like a bad token.
    # Measured: LIST -> 400 staging / 200 prod; GET?list=true -> 200 both.
    # Set for both envs so the two behave identically.
    VAULT_ADDR="$addr" VAULT_TOKEN="$tok" VAULT_STRICT_HTTP=true "$@" )
}
vault-prod() {
  _vault_with "https://vault-production.production-eks-private.internaldns-snaptravel.com" \
              VAULT_TOKEN_PROD "$@"
}
vault-staging() {
  _vault_with "https://vault-staging.private.staging.superinc.net" \
              VAULT_TOKEN_STAGING "$@"
}
